 ### [Packed Vertex Attribute Encoding](2334-packed-vertex-attribute-encoding.md)
-Packed Vertex Attribute Encoding is a compression scheme for vertex attributes in hardware-accelerated volume splatting that reduces memory usage by packing volume texture coordinates and Gaussian texture coordinates into 32-bit integers per vertex. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by specifically targeting the attribute storage of proxy geometry in EWA splatting, decoupling the coordinate components into fixed-width bit fields to achieve high compression ratios (e.g., 171:1) while allowing fast decompression via lookup tables.
+Packed Vertex Attribute Encoding is a compression scheme for vertex attributes in hardware-accelerated volume splatting that reduces memory usage by packing volume texture coordinates and Gaussian texture coordinates into 32-bit integers per vertex. It sets itself apart from floating-point attribute storage by using discrete bit-fields for metadata, allowing four vertices of a quad to share compact AO data within a single byte. This method is distinct from standard float formats by achieving significant size reduction, e.g., from 40 bytes to 12 bytes per vertex in some implementations.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. [S1]
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The DAG compression gain is highest at low levels in the tree. For an SVO with 17 levels, the second-lowest level has billions of nodes, while the DAG has at most 256 nodes (the amount of possible unique combinations of eight child voxels having each one bit) [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** For a $128K^3$ scene with 18.4 billion colored nodes, the compressed representation requires 7.63 GB of GPU memory [S1]. Real-time performance is achieved on commodity hardware for colored scenes with up to 17 hierarchical levels [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** The method supports up to 17 hierarchical levels, corresponding to a $128K^3$ voxel resolution [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Drastic memory reduction, efficient GPU storage, real-time raytracing, handles arbitrary voxel data (colors, normals, reflectance) [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Merging subtrees is difficult when attributes are tightly linked to topology (solved by decoupling). The DAG overhead (pointers per node) is higher than SVOs if not compressed effectively [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When compressing arbitrary voxel data (colors, normals) is required, and real-time raytracing on GPU is needed [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The palette compression algorithm has a time complexity of $O(n)$, where $n$ is the length of the color array [S2]. DAG construction is greedy bottom-up, which is efficient but depends on the hash/comparison cost of subtrees.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The method drastically reduces memory usage compared to uncompressed SVOs or naive colored DAGs. For a $128K^3$ scene, it uses 7.63 GB for 18.4 billion nodes [S1]. The DAG structure itself has high overhead per node (approx. 136 bits vs 40 bits in SVO) but achieves massive compression at low levels (billions of nodes reduced to at most 256) [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves real-time performance on commodity hardware for colored scenes with up to 17 hierarchical levels [S1].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Handles arbitrary attributes (colors, normals, reflectance) [S1].
+    *   Efficient GPU storage and real-time raytracing [S1].
+    *   Better than Moxel DAGs for scenes with many empty leaves due to single running sum [S1].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Decoupling introduces overhead (storing offsets/indices) [S1].
+    *   Compression gain is highest at low levels; higher levels have more unique combinations, reducing merging opportunities [S1].
+    *   Lossy compression requires tuning $e_c$ [S2].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the paper "Geometry and Attribute Compression for Voxel Scenes" by Timothy Kol et al. from Delft University of Technology [S1]. It addresses the limitation of previous DAG-based compression methods that were restricted to single-bit geometry information [S1]. The method builds on the concept of DAGs for sparse voxel scenes but extends it to arbitrary data through decoupling and palette compression [S1]. It outperforms existing state-of-the-art techniques, such as Moxel DAGs, which require more memory for indices and lack multi-resolution support [S1]. Moxel DAGs store the number of empty leaf voxels in the first child's subtree and use two running sums to compute indices, leading to large storage requirements for the index per node as empty leaves grow to quadrillions, whereas this method uses only one running sum and decouples attributes [S1].
+The method was introduced by Dolonius et al. [S1] from Delft University of Technology. It addresses the limitations of earlier DAG-based compression methods that were restricted to a single bit of geometry information per voxel [S1]. The work builds on the concept of using DAGs for sparse voxel compression and extends it to arbitrary data via decoupling and palette compression [S1]. It is used in real-time raytracing of large-scale voxel scenes [S1].
 
